@*
    A refusal, rendered where it happened and left there.

    Design §5.5: errors are not toasts. The Data API answers RFC 7807 with `title`, `detail` and a
    `type` slug, and `UnhonouredFeatures` adds a `fix` — a toast that disappears in three seconds
    throws all four away, in a tool whose whole job is configuration.

    A screen hands over the `AdminProblem` it already classified, by cascading it around the panel:
    the classification needs the site the screen caught it at, and `AdminProblem` is internal, which a
    component's public parameter may not be. Only a raw exception — what the layout's `ErrorBoundary`
    catches while rendering — is classified here. Either way a documented refusal gets a mapped headline
    over the framework's own sentence, and anything else gets a generic one, because the
    `Exception.Message` of an unexpected exception is written for a log and an operator is not reading a
    log. What is never rewritten is a framework sentence: a refusal's own `Consequence` and `Fix` are
    served verbatim (§4.1).
*@
<div class="a-error" role="alert" data-testid="error-panel">
    <div class="a-error__title" data-testid="error-title">@_text.Headline</div>
    <div class="a-error__detail">@_text.Detail</div>
    @if (_text.Fix is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <div class="a-error__fix">@_text.Fix</div>
    }
    @if (Slug is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <code class="a-error__type">@Slug</code>
    }

    @* A caller the management surface does not admit sees this on every screen, and the two
       reasons it happens need opposite answers. Either the account genuinely holds no level — the
       sentence above is then the whole story — or the session outlived the account it names, which
       is what a developer gets every time they delete the database and restart. The second is
       indistinguishable from the first from here, and it strands an operator on a screen with no
       control on it, so the way out is offered rather than left to be found in a menu. *@
    @if (_text.OffersSignOut)
    {
        <form method="post" action="@AlvoAdmin.SignOutEndpoint" data-enhance="false"
              class="a-error__actions">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <button type="submit" class="a-btn a-btn--sm" data-testid="forbidden-sign-out">
                Sign out and sign in again
            </button>
        </form>
    }

    @if (Actions is not null)
    {
        <div class="a-row a-error__actions">@Actions</div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// An exception nothing has classified — what an <c>ErrorBoundary</c> caught while rendering. A screen
    /// that caught and classified its own cascades the <c>AdminProblem</c> instead.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Exception? Fault { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The headline, when the caller has a better one than the exception type gives.</summary>
    /// <remarks>Named <c>Title</c> to match the vocabulary every other panel-like component uses
    /// (docs/architecture/admin-dashboard-review.md, F-7), rather than this component's own
    /// <c>Heading</c>.</remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What to do about it — served verbatim when it came from the framework.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Fix { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The problem-type slug, when one applies.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Slug { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the operator can do from here, drawn under the panel's text.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The screen's own classification, site and all; see <see cref="ErrorPanelText"/>.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter]
    private AdminProblem? Classified { get; set; }

    private ErrorPanelText _text = ErrorPanelText.Of(null, null, null, null);

    /// <summary>
    /// Projected once per parameter set rather than per read. An exception the classifier drops still gets
    /// the generic panel here: a panel was asked for, so it must say something.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _text = ErrorPanelText.Of(Classified, Fault, Title, Fix);
}
